          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Does Creatine Cause Water Retention or Kidney Damage?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Creatine can cause a small, early gain in body weight from water stored inside muscle, but in healthy people the research has not shown that it damages the kidneys. Both concerns are among the most common myths the ISSN reviews address directly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water retention</h3>

          <p>
            The weight gain usually comes in the first week, especially with loading; the <a href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s12970-017-0173-z" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">ISSN position stand on creatine (Kreider et al., 2017)</a> reports about 1 to 2 kg in that phase. That water sits inside muscle cells along with the extra creatine, rather than under the skin, so it is not the puffy bloating people picture. The <a href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s12970-021-00412-w" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">2021 ISSN review of common creatine questions (Antonio et al.)</a> concludes that longer-term studies do not show creatine increasing total body water relative to muscle mass. If you weigh in for a sport, plan any loading phase well away from competition, or skip it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Kidneys and other myths</h3>

          <p>
            Creatine raises the level of creatinine in blood, a breakdown product that doctors use as a marker of kidney function. That can make a routine blood test look worse even when the kidneys are fine, so tell your doctor you take it. The <a href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s12970-021-00412-w" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">2021 ISSN review of common creatine questions (Antonio et al.)</a> states that studies at recommended doses have not found kidney harm in healthy people, and that the evidence also does not support claims that creatine causes dehydration, cramping or hair loss. Protein powder raises similar questions; our page on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> covers high-protein diets and kidney health.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">HEALTH CAVEAT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check with a doctor first if any of these apply.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before taking creatine or adding much more protein if you have kidney or liver disease, diabetes, high blood pressure, or take medications that affect the kidneys, such as some diuretics and NSAIDs. Ask first if you are pregnant or breastfeeding. Under-18s should speak to a doctor before using creatine. This page is general information, not medical advice.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For healthy adults, the bigger practical risks are product quality and dosing. Choose plain creatine monohydrate rather than exotic forms, and look for a third-party certification such as NSF Certified for Sport if you are drug-tested.
          </p>
        </div>

