        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Creatine and Which Protein Powder Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy plain creatine monohydrate, 5 g a serving, with a sport certification if you are tested, and buy whichever protein powder gives you 20 to 30 g of protein at the lowest cost per 25 g that your stomach tolerates. Fancy versions of either rarely add anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            For creatine, monohydrate is the form with the research behind it. The <a href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s12970-017-0173-z" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">ISSN position stand on creatine (Kreider et al., 2017)</a> notes that other forms, such as creatine ethyl ester or buffered creatine, have not been shown to work better, and they usually cost more. Thorne Creatine is NSF Certified for Sport per the brand, and the Nutricost Creapure tub gives you the Creapure raw material at about $0.38 a serving. Optimum Nutrition's micronized tub is cheapest but we could not confirm a current sport certification for it. Our <a href="/best-creatine-monohydrate/">best creatine monohydrate</a> guide compares more tubs and explains why creatine gummies are a gamble.
          </p>

          <p>
            For protein, whey is the cheapest complete protein for most people. Gold Standard 100% Whey is the value default at about $1.49 per 25 g at the price we saw, and the <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition review</a> covers its flavors and sizes. Dymatize ISO100 costs a little more but is a whey isolate with very little lactose; see our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize review</a> and the wider <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> list. If you avoid dairy, a pea or soy blend with around 25 g per serving works too, and plant-based eaters often benefit most from creatine because their diets contain little of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, read the label for the dose per serving, the protein per scoop and any certification, and check the brand page or certifier database rather than marketing copy. Thorne also makes protein; the <a href="/thorne-protein-powder-review/">Thorne protein review</a> covers it, and the <a href="/nutricost-protein-powder-review/">Nutricost review</a> covers that brand's whey.
          </p>
