        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating creatine as a protein replacement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Creatine contains no protein and no calories, so swapping your shake for a scoop of creatine leaves you short of the amino acids muscle needs. If you are under about 1.4 g of protein per kg of body weight, creatine cannot fix that. Hit your protein target first, then add creatine for the strength and power benefit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking creatine only on training days</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Creatine works by keeping muscle stores full, not by acting in the hour after you take it. Skipping rest days lets stores drift down slowly and wastes some of the benefit. Take 3 to 5 g every day, including weekends and rest days, and attach it to a habit such as breakfast so you do not forget.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on an underdosed protein-plus-creatine blend</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some protein powders with creatine add only 1 to 3 g per serving, below the 3 to 5 g daily range used in the research, and a few hide the amount in a proprietary blend. Check the label for the exact creatine figure. If it is low or missing, a separate plain tub is cheaper and more reliable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Panicking about early weight gain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gaining 1 to 2 kg in the first week of creatine, especially after loading, is mostly water stored inside muscle, not fat. Stopping creatine because the scale moved throws away the benefit. Judge progress on strength and how your clothes fit over several weeks, and skip loading if a quick jump bothers you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring health conditions and blood tests</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Creatine raises blood creatinine, a kidney marker, which can make a routine test look abnormal. Tell your doctor you take it before blood work. Anyone with kidney disease, diabetes, kidney-affecting medications, pregnancy, or under 18 should get medical advice before starting creatine or raising protein intake sharply.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is creatine better than protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Neither is better overall because they do different jobs. Creatine monohydrate adds strength and power by raising muscle phosphocreatine, while protein powder is a food that helps you reach your daily protein target. If you already eat about 1.6 g of protein per kg of body weight, creatine adds more. If you fall short, protein powder fixes the bigger gap first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I mix creatine with my protein shake?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Stirring 3 to 5 g of unflavored creatine monohydrate into a protein shake is a convenient way to take both, and there is no known interaction between them. The ISSN creatine review notes that taking creatine with carbohydrate or protein may slightly improve how much muscle retains. Just remember to take creatine on rest days too, even without a shake.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does creatine make you gain weight?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Creatine often adds about 1 to 2 kg in the first week, mostly water held inside muscle cells, according to the ISSN position stand. Over months, any further weight gain mostly reflects added muscle from harder training. Creatine contains no calories, so it does not cause fat gain. If you compete in a weight class, avoid loading close to a weigh-in.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do I need protein powder if I take creatine?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Only if you cannot reach your daily protein target from food. Creatine does not supply protein, so you still need about 1.4 to 2.0 g per kg of body weight a day if you train hard, per the ISSN protein position stand. Meat, fish, eggs, dairy and legumes can cover that. Protein powder is simply a convenient, inexpensive way to close the gap.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is creatine bad for your kidneys?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  In healthy people taking recommended doses, research reviews including the 2017 ISSN position stand and a 2021 ISSN paper on creatine myths have not found kidney damage. Creatine does raise blood creatinine, which can affect kidney test results, so tell your doctor. People with kidney disease or taking kidney-affecting medications should get medical advice before using it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should I take creatine before or after a workout?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It matters little. Some small studies suggest a slight edge for taking creatine after training, but reviews conclude daily consistency is far more important than timing, because creatine works by keeping muscle stores full. Taking 5 g with your post-workout protein shake is an easy routine, and on rest days take it with any meal you never skip.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can teenagers take creatine or protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Teenagers can usually meet their protein needs from food, and protein powder is a food, so a shake is generally fine as part of a balanced diet. Creatine is different: most research involves adults, and many sports bodies advise caution for under-18s. A teenager should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before using creatine.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

