          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Creatine vs protein powder is not a contest. Creatine monohydrate is a 3 to 5 g daily supplement that fills your muscles' phosphocreatine stores and adds strength, power and training volume for about $0.23 to $0.49 a day. Protein powder is a food that helps you reach 1.4 to 2.0 g of protein per kg of body weight, at about $1.49 to $1.67 per 25 g at the Amazon prices we saw. The ISSN position stands back both.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Take creatine if your protein intake is already covered, take protein powder if it is not, and take both if you train hard and want the two supplements with the most evidence behind them. Mix them in one shake, take creatine every day, and talk to a doctor first if you have kidney disease, take medication or are under 18. For tub-by-tub picks, see our <a href="/best-creatine-monohydrate/">best creatine monohydrate</a> rankings.
          </p>
